## Proof

Let $V = \{p \in \mathbb{R}[x] : \deg p \le n\}$, which is a vector space of dimension $n+1$. Define
$$N(p) = \max\{|p(a_j)| : 0 \le j \le k\},$$
where $a_0, a_1, \dots, a_k$ are distinct real numbers.

We determine when $N$ is a norm on $V$.

### Norm axioms

A function $N: V \to \mathbb{R}$ is a norm if it satisfies:

1. **Positive definiteness**: $N(p) \ge 0$ for all $p$, and $N(p) = 0 \iff p = 0$.
2. **Absolute homogeneity**: $N(\lambda p) = |\lambda|\, N(p)$ for all $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, $p \in V$.
3. **Subadditivity (triangle inequality)**: $N(p+q) \le N(p) + N(q)$.

### Axioms 2 and 3 always hold

For any $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$:
$$N(\lambda p) = \max_j |\lambda\, p(a_j)| = |\lambda| \max_j |p(a_j)| = |\lambda|\, N(p).$$

For any $p, q \in V$, using the triangle inequality for $|\cdot|$ at each point:
$$N(p+q) = \max_j |p(a_j) + q(a_j)| \le \max_j \big(|p(a_j)| + |q(a_j)|\big) \le N(p) + N(q).$$

Also $N(p) \ge 0$ is immediate since $N$ is a max of nonneg values. So the only question is **definiteness**: when does $N(p) = 0$ imply $p = 0$?

### Analysis of definiteness

$N(p) = 0$ means $|p(a_j)| = 0$ for every $j = 0, \dots, k$, i.e., $p(a_j) = 0$ for all $j$. Thus $p$ vanishes at the $k+1$ distinct points $a_0, \dots, a_k$.

**Case $k \ge n$ (i.e., $k+1 \ge n+1$ points).** A nonzero polynomial of degree $\le n$ has at most $n$ real roots. If $p$ vanishes at $k+1 \ge n+1$ distinct points, then $p$ has at least $n+1$ roots, which exceeds $n$. Hence $p \equiv 0$. So $N(p) = 0 \implies p = 0$, and definiteness holds.

**Case $k < n$ (i.e., $k+1 \le n$ points).** The polynomial
$$r(x) = \prod_{j=0}^{k}(x - a_j)$$
has degree $k+1 \le n$, so $r \in V$, and $r \neq 0$, yet $r(a_j) = 0$ for all $j$, giving $N(r) = 0$. So $N$ is not definite, hence not a norm.

### Conclusion

The function $N(p) = \max\{|p(a_j)| : 0 \le j \le k\}$ is a norm on $V$ **if and only if $k \ge n$**, i.e., the number of evaluation points $k+1$ is at least the dimension $n+1$ of the space $V$.

